  "final_text": "I've built the dashboard and the alert rule. The rule is enabled, and its first run succeeded with no alerts firing.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The `Kibana Sample Data Logs` data view (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`) contains web server access logs, one document per request, and uses `timestamp` as its time field. Each request records:\n- **Visitor:** `clientip`, `agent` (browser), `machine.os`\n- **Request:** `request`, `url`, `extension`, `host`, `referer`\n- **Result:** `response` (HTTP status code, e.g. 200/404/503) and `bytes` (response size)\n- **Location:** `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`\n- **Other:** `tags`, `memory`, `phpmemory`, `machine.ram`\n\nThe text fields have `.keyword` versions, which are the ones charts can group by.\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-415c192b Web logs overview\"** (id `c4ee2091-ce09-432a-848a-957b7942c9e3`, default time range the last 7 days). It has 8 panels:\n- Four summary numbers: total requests, unique visitors (distinct `clientip`), average response size in bytes, and the count of 5xx errors\n- Requests over time, as stacked bars split by response code\n- A donut chart of requests by response code\n- A table of the top 10 requested URLs, with request count and total bytes\n- A table of the top 10 source countries (`geo.src`)\n\nThe API accepted all 8 panels, but I haven't opened the dashboard in the browser to check how they display.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-415c192b 5xx spike\"** (id `bc072b0b-28da-429b-8a1f-f643c45e8342`). It's an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute. It counts requests whose response code starts with \"5\" (5xx) over the last 5 minutes and fires when the count is above 10. It has no notification actions, so when it fires the alert only shows up in Kibana. Add a connector (email, Slack, etc.) if you want to be notified.",
